// RV32I regression program for the 5-STAGE PIPELINED core, WITH FORWARDING.
// Loaded by instruction_Memory.v via $readmemh; one 32-bit word per line.
// Expected final register state is asserted in Pipeline_Top_TestBench.v.
//
//=============================================================================
// WHY THIS PROGRAM IS NOT THE SAME FILE AS single_core/program.hex
//
// It computes exactly the same 12 results, in the same order, into the same
// registers. That is on purpose: the two cores must reach an IDENTICAL
// architectural outcome, so the same 12 checks can be used on both.
//
// What differs is the SCHEDULE. This version has EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding
// (see Execute_Cycle.v) and needs ZERO data-hazard NOPs - down from 3 in the
// pre-forwarding version of this file. The 2 branch-delay-slot NOPs are still
// here: forwarding only fixes DATA hazards, and the branch-delay problem is a
// CONTROL hazard - a completely different mechanism, unaffected by this
// change. Fixing that needs flush logic, which is not built yet.
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY FORWARDING NEEDS ZERO NOPs FOR MOST OF THIS PROGRAM
//
// Execute_Cycle.v's forwarding unit compares the register EX is about to read
// against the register the instruction in MEM is about to write (RdM) and the
// register the instruction in WB is about to write (RdW), and grabs the value
// straight off ALU_ResultM or ResultW if either matches. That covers a
// producer sitting 1 instruction back (EX/MEM) or 2 instructions back
// (MEM/WB) - see the FORWARDING UNIT comment in Execute_Cycle.v for exactly
// how those two cases are told apart, and for the priority rule when both
// would match.
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE GAP THIS DESIGN DOES *NOT* CLOSE - A PRODUCER EXACTLY 3 SLOTS BACK
//
// Forwarding reaches back 2 stages (EX/MEM, MEM/WB). Reading straight from
// the register file is only safe once a producer is >=4 slots back (the exact
// derivation is preserved below, for reference). That leaves a real hole at
// EXACTLY 3 slots back: too far for either forwarding path, too close for an
// unassisted register-file read.
//
// Concretely: Register_file.v writes on the rising edge with no write-through
// bypass, and Decode_Cycle.v's ID/EX register latches its read with a
// non-blocking assignment on THAT SAME EDGE when producer p and consumer
// c=p+3 line up. Verilog's non-blocking-assignment semantics guarantee the
// read side samples the PRE-edge (stale) value in that exact collision - so a
// producer 3 slots back is silently wrong, not silently safe.
//
// The textbook fix (Harris & Harris, Patterson & Hennessy) is a register file
// that WRITES in the first half of the clock cycle and READS in the second
// half, so a same-cycle write-then-read inside one clock period just works.
// This project deliberately does NOT do that: Register_file.v is shared with
// single_core/ and is being kept frozen (see the include-strategy note in
// Pipeline_Top.v) - changing its write timing risks the single-cycle
// regression for a benefit that only matters here. So this program is simply
// SCHEDULED to never place a dependent instruction exactly 3 slots behind its
// producer. See the per-instruction distance notes below - every one lands on
// 1, 2 (forwarded) or >=4 (already committed), and none lands on 3.
//
//   (When hazard-detection + stalling is built next, a load-use stall makes
//   this gap irrelevant for lw specifically. A general producer-exactly-3-back
//   ALU hazard remains a real, documented limitation of this design until the
//   register file itself is changed - which is why it is called out here
//   explicitly rather than swept under the schedule.)
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ORIGINAL (PRE-FORWARDING) 3-NOP DERIVATION, KEPT FOR REFERENCE
//
//   Let each instruction be numbered by its slot index i (0,1,2,...). With one
//   instruction issued per cycle and a 100 ns clock:
//
//     instruction i is in  IF  during  (100i, 100i+100)
//                          ID  during  (100i+100, 100i+200)
//                          EX  during  (100i+200, 100i+300)
//                          MEM during  (100i+300, 100i+400)
//                          WB  during  (100i+400, 100i+500)
//
//   producer p commits (posedge, end of WB):      t = 100p + 600
//   consumer c samples (posedge, end of ID):      t = 100c + 300
//
//   Direct register-file read is safe only if the write lands STRICTLY before
//   the sample:  100p+600 < 100c+300  =>  c > p+3  =>  c >= p+4.
//   That is where "3 NOPs" and "distance must be >=4" both come from.
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HAZARD 2 - CONTROL HAZARD: a taken branch still needs 2 delay-slot NOPs
//
//   A branch is resolved in EX (PCSrcE = BranchE & ZeroE, see
//   Execute_Cycle.v). By the time PCSrcE is valid, fetch has already fetched
//   the two instructions physically following the branch in memory. This
//   build has no flush logic, so those two instructions WILL execute -
//   forwarding has nothing to do with this, and does not touch it. The two
//   slots immediately after a taken branch remain DELAY SLOTS filled with
//   NOPs. (Real MIPS made this part of its ISA and let compilers fill the
//   slot with useful work.) A NOT-taken branch needs nothing extra.
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE NOP ENCODING
//   0x00000013 is  addi x0, x0, 0  - the canonical RISC-V NOP. It writes to x0,
//   which is hardwired to zero, so the write is architecturally invisible. It
//   is a real instruction that flows through all five stages like any other.
//=============================================================================
//
// idx  addr   encoding   instruction          | why it is here
00500093   // 0   0x00   addi x1, x0, 5        | x1 = 5
00300113   // 1   0x04   addi x2, x0, 3        | x2 = 3
002081b3   // 2   0x08   add  x3, x1, x2       | x3 = 8. rs1=x1 is 2 back from
           //                                  |  its producer (idx0) -> MEM/WB
           //                                  |  forward. rs2=x2 is 1 back
           //                                  |  from its producer (idx1) ->
           //                                  |  EX/MEM forward. ZERO NOPs -
           //                                  |  this line is the proof that
           //                                  |  both forwarding paths work.
00302023   // 3   0x0c   sw   x3, 0(x0)        | mem[0] = 8. rs2=x3 is 1 back
           //                                  |  from its producer (idx2) ->
           //                                  |  EX/MEM forward INTO THE
           //                                  |  STORE-DATA PATH specifically
           //                                  |  (WriteDataM <= ForwardedRD2E
           //                                  |  in Execute_Cycle.v). Without
           //                                  |  that specific fix this line
           //                                  |  would silently store 0, not
           //                                  |  8, and the lw check below
           //                                  |  would fail - this is the
           //                                  |  line that proves it.
00002483   // 4   0x10   lw   x9, 0(x0)        | x9 = 8. Memory hazard (not a
           //                                  |  register hazard): the store
           //                                  |  commits at the edge ending
           //                                  |  its MEM cycle, this load
           //                                  |  reads combinationally during
           //                                  |  the cycle right after. Works
           //                                  |  with 0 gap regardless of
           //                                  |  register forwarding.
40208233   // 5   0x14   sub  x4, x1, x2       | x4 = 2. x1 is 5 back, x2 is 4
           //                                  |  back - both already
           //                                  |  committed to the register
           //                                  |  file (>=4, no forwarding
           //                                  |  needed). The sw/lw pair
           //                                  |  above is what supplies this
           //                                  |  spacing "for free" - no
           //                                  |  padding NOPs were needed.
0020f2b3   // 6   0x18   and  x5, x1, x2       | x5 = 5 & 3 = 1
0020e333   // 7   0x1c   or   x6, x1, x2       | x6 = 5 | 3 = 7
001123b3   // 8   0x20   slt  x7, x2, x1       | x7 = (3 < 5) = 1
0020a433   // 9   0x24   slt  x8, x1, x2       | x8 = (5 < 3) = 0
00208463   // 10  0x28   beq  x1, x2, +8       | NOT taken (5 != 3). No delay
           //                                  |  slots needed for a fall-through.
00100513   // 11  0x2c   addi x10, x0, 1       | x10 = 1 - proves fall-through
00108863   // 12  0x30   beq  x1, x1, +16      | TAKEN (5 == 5) -> 0x30+16 = 0x40
00000013   // 13  0x34   nop                   | delay slot 1 - fetched before
00000013   // 14  0x38   nop                   | delay slot 2 - the branch resolved
06300593   // 15  0x3c   addi x11, x0, 99      | MUST be skipped -> x11 stays 0
00700613   // 16  0x40   addi x12, x0, 7       | x12 = 7 - the branch target
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE ON THE BRANCH OFFSET AT idx 12
//   Same shape as the pre-forwarding version of this file, just at new
//   addresses (removing the RAW NOPs shifted everything after them earlier,
//   but never changed the SPACING between the branch and what it skips):
//
//        0x30  beq  (branch)
//        0x34  nop     <- runs anyway (delay slot)
//        0x38  nop     <- runs anyway (delay slot)
//        0x3c  addi x11, x0, 99   <- the one we actually want to skip
//        0x40  addi x12, x0, 7    <- target:  0x40 - 0x30 = 16
//
//   Encoding check for 0x00108863 against Sign_Extend.v's B-type unscramble
//   {{19{In[31]}}, In[31], In[7], In[30:25], In[11:8], 1'b0}:
//     In[31]=0, In[7]=1, In[30:25]=000000, In[11:8]=0000  ->  0_1_000000_0000_0
//     = 0b10000 = 16.  Correct - identical encoding to before, since the
//   offset is RELATIVE and the delay-slot shape around it never changed.
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE ON x11
//   x11 is never written by this program. It reads back as 0 because
//   Register_file.v clears all 32 registers on reset. If the branch silently
//   failed to redirect the PC, idx 15 would run and x11 would become 99 -
//   which is precisely what makes this a real test of branch behaviour rather
//   than a test that passes by accident.
//=============================================================================
